WHEREAS the Insurance Bureau of Canada has reported that insured losses from severe weather events have increased from an annual average of approximately $700 million between 2001 and 2010 to a record $8.5 billion in 2024; and
WHEREAS the property and casualty insurance sector is actively engaged with governments and municipalities across Canada through initiatives related to flood-risk management, climate adaptation, resilience planning, risk analysis, loss prevention, and municipal risk-management programs intended to reduce the human and financial impacts of extreme weather events;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that staff, as part of ongoing work related to flood resilience, climate adaptation, stormwater management, and disaster mitigation, explore opportunities to engage representatives of the property and casualty insurance sector, including organizations such as the Insurance Bureau of Canada, as well as through the Association of Ontario Municipalities (AMO), and report back through future relevant reports, where appropriate, on emerging best practices, partnerships, funding opportunities, risk-analysis tools, and data sources that may assist municipalities in reducing flood-related risks and losses.

WHEREAS extreme weather events are becoming increasingly frequent and severe, requiring the City to enhance its emergency preparedness, response, and recovery capabilities;
WHEREAS on July 1, 2026, the City of Ottawa experienced an unprecedented extreme weather event with Britannia recording 167 mm of rain—a volume that exceeded a 1-in-200-year event;
WHEREAS the severity of the extreme weather event led to widespread flooding and significant property damage, resulting in more than 5,700 service requests related to flooding and weather impacts (as of July 13, 2026), with the most impacted neighbourhoods in Wards 7,8,9, and 4;
WHEREAS City staff provided updates and outreach to individual Councillors representing impacted neighbourhood’s following the July 1, 2026 extreme weather event, and a coordinated operational recovery briefing,  bringing together Councillors  from the most significantly impacted wards, was held on July 7, six days following the event on July 1st, and
WHEREAS Community Information Hubs were set up on July 9, eight days after the major weather event, to support residents affected by flooding and assist with recovery needs; and
WHEREAS major weather events are dynamic situations where the nature and scale of impacts, resident needs, and recovery requirements continue to evolve following the initial emergency response, requiring flexible and coordinated approaches across City services, community partners, and all levels of government;
WHEREAS the City’s Solid Waste Services department responded to support residents through  targeted special collections in impacted neighbourhoods, timely adjustments to organic waste collection and operational flexibility to support residents with cleanup following the event,
WHEREAS questions have arisen regarding the coordination of communications, resident supports , volunteer coordination, and recovery services provided by the City and community partners,
WHEREAS effective emergency management requires coordinated planning and action across the full emergency management cycle, including preparedness, response, and recovery, with clear processes to support the transition from immediate emergency response operations to longer-term community recovery.
WHEREAS Ontario has specific emergency management requirements set out under provincial legislation and regulations that are mandatory for municipalities as per the Emergency Management and Civil Protection Act (EMCPA),
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that Council direct staff to undertake a comprehensive review of the City’s emergency response and recovery functions following the July 1, 2026 extreme weather event, including a review of emergency management protocols, and coordinated response procedures to ensure alignment with the requirements of the Emergency Management and Civil Protection Act (EMCPA) and that the review include:
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED that this review include specific recommendations to improve coordination between City Staff, community partners and Members of Council throughout the recovery from an extreme weather event.

WHEREAS the Transportation and Mobility Strategy and Action Plan identifies a number of operational measures intended to improve mobility, reduce congestion, and enhance the reliability of the City's transportation network in response to increased travel demand; and
WHEREAS many of these measures represent sound transportation management practices that would benefit residents beyond the current return-to-office transition period; and
WHEREAS proactive traffic management, coordinated construction planning, intelligent transportation systems, real-time traffic monitoring, transit priority, timely public communications, and effective interdepartmental coordination contribute to a more efficient and resilient transportation network; and
WHEREAS staff are reporting back through the Transportation and Mobility Strategy and Action Plan update in Q1 2027, identifying which operational measures implemented through the Transportation and Mobility Strategy and Action Plan could be discontinued, maintained or incorporated as regular business practices on a permanent basis;
WHEREAS the City's Transportation System Management Strategy, approved by Council in 2011, is currently being updated with planned completion by the end of 2027; and
WHEREAS the Transportation System Management Strategy update will provide an opportunity to incorporate any lessons learned through the implementation of the Transportation and Mobility Strategy and Action Plan to enhance proactive traffic management and coordination;
THERFORE BE IT RESOLVED that the Transportation System Management Strategy update report include recommendations to strengthen governance, coordination, and accountability for transportation and mobility across the Corporation, including options for a clear decision-making framework with defined responsibility and authority for overseeing the City's mobility network;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED that the Transportation System Management Strategy update report identify any policy, operational, technology, staffing, or funding requirements necessary to support the long-term implementation of these measures; and
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED that as part of the Transportation System Management Strategy update report, staff identify opportunities for future investment, including but not limited to intelligent transportation systems, adaptive traffic signal technology, traffic monitoring and analytics, incident management tools, transit priority technologies, traveller information systems, and other mobility initiatives, for consideration through future budget deliberations and long-range financial planning.

